Kino-Gauze
Ingredients: KRAUTROCK + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + RHODES MK1 RETRO LATE-NIGHT SOUL + 1960s ERA
Creative Catalyst: In shuttered cinema booths across late-1960s West Germany, rogue projectionists jammed on discarded instruments to soundtrack silent movies that only existed in their sleep-deprived minds.

Kino-Gauze is the spectral collision of hypnotic, motorik-driven West German experimental rock and the smoldering, velvet-lit melancholy of late-night 1960s American soul. Propelled by relentless, metronomic drum rhythms that feel like film clicking relentlessly through sprockets, the sound is washed in the warm, bell-like decay of a Rhodes MK1 electric piano drowned in optical tremolo and cavernous plate reverberation. Ghostly soul vocals, recorded with ribbon microphones through cloudy valve preamps, drift through eerie tape delays and phantom projector noise, creating an intoxicating trance state that feels both intimately nostalgic and deeply disquieting.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 104–116 BPM; a disciplined, hypnotic, four-on-the-floor "motorik" pulse with rigid forward locomotion, deliberately softened and counterbalanced by lazy, behind-the-beat soul basslines and dragging ride cymbals.
- Harmonic Palette: Minor 7th, minor 9th, and half-diminished chords rooted in Dorian and Aeolian modes, spiced with chromatic descending bass walks straight out of late-1960s Stax/Muscle Shoals ballads, cast into eerie suspension.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Optical Tremolo & Spring Immersion: Rhodes MK1 run through choppy optical tremolo pedals and tube-driven spring reverb tanks, creating an aquatic, pulsing shimmer.
  2. Damped Mono Drum Tracking: Vintage 1960s drum kits muffled with heavy wool felt and recorded with a single overhead ribbon mic to produce a dry, hollow, hypnotic thrum.
  3. Celluloid Artifact Bleed: Genuine mechanical artifacts—the faint chatter of a 16mm gate, carbon-arc hum, and delicate tape-head wow/flutter—mixed beneath the music like spectral percussion.
- Dynamics: Long, trance-inducing structural vamps that maintain unchanging rhythmic momentum while instrument layers phase, filter, and saturate over ten-minute stretches, abruptly terminating in sudden "reel-end" tape cuts.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement materialized between 1968 and 1971 in the unventilated projection rooms of decaying pre-war film palaces in Munich and West Berlin. After the patrons filed out into the wet cobblestone streets, graveyard-shift projectionists would thread blank white film or scratched leader tape through their 35mm projectors to illuminate the cavernous, empty auditoriums. Hauling up pawnshop instruments—most notably battered Rhodes MK1 pianos discarded by departing Allied military clubs—these renegade operators improvised marathon musical suites to hallucinated films. The subculture adopted a stark sartorial uniform of grease-stained wool shop coats, narrow black ties, dark turtlenecks, and horn-rimmed safety spectacles, smelling of paraffin wax, cold coffee, and photographic developer. Their clandestine concerts, dubbed Spulenfeste (Spool Festivals), permitted no stage lighting; audiences sat in velvet cinema seats watching empty screens while the band performed unseen from high behind the projection glass, sending ghostly soul vamps echoing across rows of red upholstery.

Central Instruments: Rhodes MK1 Electric Piano (heavily tremoloed), 1960s Ludwig Club Date Drum Kit (tea-towel damped), Höfner 500/1 "Violin" Bass with flatwound strings, Dynacord Echocord tape-delay unit, Gibson ES-330 hollow-body guitar through a Fender Princeton Reverb, and modified Siemens 16mm film projectors used as mechanical drone oscillators.

